{"name":"Sorrel Felt","hex":"#a49688","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sorrel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any member of species of the genus Rumex of various plants with acidic leaves, especially\ncommon sorrel or garden sorrel (Rumex acetosa), sometimes used as a salad vegetable.","common sorrel or garden sorrel (Rumex acetosa), sometimes used as a salad vegetable."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A brown colour, with a tint of red."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sorrel"},{"term":"felt","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cloth or stuff made of matted fibres of wool, or wool and fur, fulled or wrought into a compact substance by rolling and pressure, with lees or size, without spinning or weaving.","A hat made of felt."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make into felt or a feltlike substance; to cause to adhere and mat together.","To cover with, or as if with, felt."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/felt"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sorrel","blocks":[{"text":"Sorrel is a perennial herbaceous plant in the family Polygonaceae. It is also called common sorrel, meadow sorrel, green sorrel, sour dock, spinach dock and narrow-leaved dock."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/%28MHNT%29_Rumex_acetosa_-_Habit.jpg/330px-%28MHNT%29_Rumex_acetosa_-_Habit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sorrel","wikidata":"Q26297"},{"title":"Felt","blocks":[{"text":"Felt is a textile that is produced by matting, condensing, and pressing fibers together. Felt can be made of natural fibers such as wool or animal fur, or from synthetic fibers such as petroleum-based acrylic or acrylonitrile or wood pulp–based rayon. Blended fibers are also common. Natural fiber felt has special properties that allow it to be used for a wide variety of purposes. It is \"fire-retardant and self-extinguishing; it dampen